A crate is either a *binary* (has a `main.rs`, produces an .exe, like `atlas-server`) or a *library* (has a `lib.rs`, produces code other crates import, like `graph-types`). Its name and dependencies are declared in `Cargo.toml`. Inside a library crate, `pub` items become that crate's public API to everyone else.

Example:
```
// graph-types/src/lib.rs
pub mod id;
pub mod graph;
pub use graph::Graph;
```
A newcomer's mistake: assuming one crate == one file. A crate is a whole directory tree rooted at `src/lib.rs` or `src/main.rs`; `mod` lines (see `module`) wire the rest of the tree in.

`mod name;` tells the compiler to compile `name.rs` (or `name/mod.rs`) as part of the current module tree; `mod name { ... }` declares one inline instead. You reach into a module with `::`, e.g. `crate::id::NodeId`, or bring a path into scope with `use`.

Example:
```
// lib.rs
pub mod id;
pub mod graph;

// graph.rs
use crate::id::AnyNodeId;
```
Newcomer mistake: forgetting `mod x;` in the parent — Rust does not auto-discover files the way some languages auto-discover folders; an un-declared file simply isn't part of the crate and won't even show as an error until you try to `use` it.

`pub trait Name { fn method(&self) -> Type; }` declares the promise; `impl Name for ConcreteType { ... }` fulfills it. Traits can have default method bodies, associated types, and associated constants.

Example:
```
pub trait KindTag {
    const KIND: NodeKind;
}
```
Newcomer mistake: thinking a trait alone is a usable type. You can't hold 'a KindTag' directly — you hold a concrete type that implements it, or a `Box<dyn Trait>` (see `Box`) for a dynamically-chosen implementer.

`impl TypeName { fn method(&self) -> ... }` adds inherent methods; `impl TraitName for TypeName { ... }` fulfills a trait's promise for that type. A single type can have several `impl` blocks, even in different files.

Example:
```
impl<K: KindTag> NodeId<K> {
    pub fn new(raw: impl Into<Interned>) -> Self {
        NodeId(raw.into(), PhantomData)
    }
}
```
Newcomer mistake: expecting inheritance. There's no `class Dog : Animal` in Rust — shared behavior comes from multiple types implementing the same trait, not from subclassing.

Written as `T: TraitName` in the angle brackets, or after a `where` clause for readability with several bounds.

Example:
```
pub trait KindTag {
    const KIND: NodeKind;
}

impl<K: KindTag> NodeId<K> {
    pub fn new(raw: impl Into<Interned>) -> Self { ... }
}
```
`impl Into<Interned>` in the parameter position is a shorthand trait bound too ('any type that can convert into an Interned'). Newcomer mistake: forgetting the bound and then being confused why `K::KIND` or `k.some_method()` doesn't compile inside a generic function — without `K: KindTag`, the compiler only knows `K` is SOME type, not what it can do.

Only usable inside a function that itself returns `Result` (or `Option`) with a compatible error type. `expr?` is shorthand for: if `expr` is `Err(e)`, `return Err(e.into())` immediately; if `Ok(v)`, the whole expression evaluates to `v`.

Example:
```
pub fn compile(raw_dir: &Path) -> anyhow::Result<AtlasData> {
    let kjv_raw = read(&raw_dir.join("kjv.json"))?;
    let (canon, verses) = kjv::parse(&kjv_raw)?;
    // ...
}
```
Each `?` bails out of `compile` the moment any one step fails. Newcomer mistake: trying to use `?` in `fn main()` without `main` itself returning a `Result` — as of modern Rust, `fn main() -> anyhow::Result<()>` fixes this, which is exactly the pattern this codebase's binaries use.

```
match self {
    RelationId::Contains => ...,
    RelationId::Attests => ...,
    // every variant, or a `_ => ...` catch-all
}
```
Each arm can also destructure data out of the matched value (see `pattern-matching`). Newcomer mistake: reaching for a catch-all `_ => ...` arm out of habit — it compiles, but it silently swallows any FUTURE variant you add, defeating the exhaustiveness check that's `match`'s whole safety benefit.

```
if let Some(n) = &b {
    // use n
}
```
equivalent to a `match` with one real arm and a `_ => {}` arm, but shorter. Pair with `else` for the negative case, or `while let` to loop while a pattern keeps matching. Newcomer mistake: chaining several unrelated `if let`s where a `match` would be clearer — `if let` shines for ONE pattern; more than that, prefer `match` so every case is visible together.

Same API shape as `HashMap` (`.insert`, `.get`), but keys must implement `Ord` (see `Ord-PartialOrd`) rather than just `Hash`.

Example:
```
pub struct Graph {
    pub nodes: BTreeMap<AnyNodeId, Node>,
    // ...
}
```
Newcomer mistake: defaulting to `BTreeMap` everywhere 'to be safe' — it's the right choice when you serialize, hash, or diff the contents; a `HashMap` is fine (and slightly faster) for purely in-memory, never-compared lookups.

`Mutex::new(value)`, then `.lock()` to get exclusive access (blocks until available, returns a guard that unlocks automatically when dropped).

Example (illustrative, not from this repo):
```
use std::sync::Mutex;
let counter = Mutex::new(0);
*counter.lock().unwrap() += 1;
```
Newcomer mistake: holding a lock longer than necessary (e.g., across an `.await` or a slow computation), which blocks every other thread waiting on it — lock, do the minimum, unlock.

```
macro_rules! kind_tags {
    ($($tag:ident => $kind:ident),+ $(,)?) => {$(
        #[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, PartialOrd, Ord, Hash)]
        pub struct $tag;
        impl KindTag for $tag { const KIND: NodeKind = NodeKind::$kind; }
    )+};
}
```
called as `kind_tags! { PlaceTag => Place, PersonTag => Person, ... }`. Newcomer mistake: reaching for a macro before trying generics or a plain function — macros are harder to read, debug, and get IDE support for, so this codebase (like most Rust code) uses them sparingly, only where the alternative is genuinely repetitive boilerplate across TYPES (which generics can't abstract over).

`struct Name(InnerType);` — access the inner value with `.0`. Often paired with `derive` to forward useful traits, and sometimes with `impl Deref` to make the wrapper behave like its inner type where convenient.

Example:
```
pub struct ArtifactError(pub String);

impl std::fmt::Display for ArtifactError {
    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut std::fmt::Formatter) -> std::fmt::Result {
        write!(f, "{}", self.0)
    }
}
```
Newcomer mistake: treating a newtype as free ceremony — its entire value is that the compiler now catches mixing it up with its inner type or with OTHER newtypes wrapping the same inner type, which a plain type alias (see `type-alias`) would NOT catch.

Achieved with `std::marker::PhantomData<T>` — a zero-sized field that 'pretends' to hold a `T` for the compiler's bookkeeping, without actually storing one.

Example:
```
pub struct NodeId<K: KindTag>(pub Interned, pub PhantomData<K>);

impl<K: KindTag> NodeId<K> {
    pub fn new(raw: impl Into<Interned>) -> Self {
        NodeId(raw.into(), PhantomData)
    }
}
```
Newcomer mistake: forgetting `PhantomData` is needed at all when a generic parameter `K` doesn't appear in any field — without it, the compiler complains the type parameter is 'unused', since it has no way to know you intend `K` as a compile-time-only marker.

`const NAME: Type = value;` (inlined at each use site, must be computable at compile time) vs `static NAME: Type = value;` (one real memory address, can be combined with `OnceLock` for lazy one-time initialization — see `OnceLock`). A trait can also declare an associated `const`.

Example:
```
pub trait KindTag {
    const KIND: NodeKind;
}
impl KindTag for PlaceTag { const KIND: NodeKind = NodeKind::Place; }
```
Newcomer mistake: using `static mut` for shared mutable global state — it's `unsafe` and almost never the right tool; `OnceLock`, `Mutex`, or `Arc` (all thread-safe) are the idiomatic replacements.

`#[derive(Default)]` generates it automatically (each field gets ITS OWN default: `0` for numbers, empty for `Vec`/`String`/`HashMap`, etc.); `Default::default()` or `Type::default()` calls it. `..Default::default()` in struct-update syntax lets you override just a few fields and default the rest.

Example:
```
#[derive(Debug, Default)]
pub struct Graph {
    pub nodes: BTreeMap<AnyNodeId, Node>,
    // ...
}
```
Newcomer mistake: assuming EVERY type gets `Default` for free — it only derives automatically if every field's type also implements `Default`; a field with no sensible 'empty' value needs a manual `impl Default` or excludes the derive.

`#[derive(Debug)]` for the programmer's view, printed with `println!("{:?}", value)`. `impl std::fmt::Display for Type { fn fmt(&self, f: &mut Formatter) -> fmt::Result { write!(f, "...") } }` for the human-facing message, printed with `println!("{}", value)`.

Example:
```
impl std::fmt::Display for ArtifactError {
    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut std::fmt::Formatter) -> std::fmt::Result {
        write!(f, "{}", self.0)
    }
}
```
Newcomer mistake: trying to `println!("{}", value)` a type that only derives `Debug` — that's a compile error; `Debug` and `Display` are separate traits, and only types with the LATTER support `{}`.

```
impl From<&str> for MyType { fn from(s: &str) -> Self { ... } }
// then automatically: let x: MyType = some_str.into();
```
Accepting `impl Into<T>` as a parameter type (rather than `T` directly) lets a function take anything convertible, converting exactly once inside.

Example:
```
pub fn new(raw: impl Into<Interned>) -> Self {
    NodeId(raw.into(), PhantomData)
}
```
Newcomer mistake: implementing `Into<TypeB> for TypeA` directly instead of `From<TypeA> for TypeB` — the standard library gives you `Into` automatically once `From` exists (a blanket impl), so implementing `From` is almost always the right direction.

`#[derive(Hash)]` (usually alongside `PartialEq, Eq`) generates a hash by combining every field's own hash. A custom `impl Hash` is only needed when you want to hash based on LESS than all the fields (e.g., ignoring a cache field that doesn't affect equality).

Example:
```
fn hash<H: Hasher>(&self, st: &mut H) {
    // custom logic
}
```
Newcomer mistake: implementing `Hash` and `PartialEq` inconsistently (hashing on fields that `PartialEq` ignores, or vice versa) — this breaks `HashMap`/`HashSet` silently, since equal-by-`PartialEq` values could then hash differently and never be found as the 'same' key.

`anyhow::Result<T>` is shorthand for `Result<T, anyhow::Error>`; `.context("doing X")` or `anyhow::Context` adds a human-readable breadcrumb to an error as it propagates; `bail!("message")` returns an error immediately.

Example:
```
use anyhow::{bail, Context, Result};

fn compile(raw_dir: &Path) -> Result<AtlasData> {
    let kjv_raw = read(&raw_dir.join("kjv.json")).context("reading kjv.json")?;
    // ...
}
```
Newcomer mistake: using `anyhow` inside a LIBRARY crate whose callers need to `match` on specific failure kinds — `anyhow::Error` erases the original type for convenience, which is exactly wrong for a library API; use a proper error enum (`thiserror`) there instead.

`///` documents the item directly below it; `//!` (note the `!`) documents the ENCLOSING item — typically used at the very top of a file to describe the whole module or crate.

Example:
```
//! Identity: kind-tagged node ids, positions, content-addressed pids.

/// Kind tag for phantom-typed ids: a cross-kind reference is a type
/// error, not a runtime surprise.
pub trait KindTag { const KIND: NodeKind; }
```
Newcomer mistake: confusing `///` (item-level, most common) with a plain `//` comment — only `///`/`//!` are picked up by documentation tooling; a plain `//` is invisible to `cargo doc` and to doc-tests.

`#[cfg(test)]` above a module or item includes it only during `cargo test`; other conditions include target OS, feature flags (see `feature-flag`), and debug-vs-release builds.

Example:
```
#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;
    // test functions here
}
```
Newcomer mistake: putting test-only helper functions OUTSIDE a `#[cfg(test)]` block 'to keep things simple' — that ships unused test-support code (and its dependencies) into the real binary; keeping it behind `#[cfg(test)]` means it costs nothing outside `cargo test`.

Cargo features: declared in `[features]` in `Cargo.toml`, checked in code with `#[cfg(feature = "name")]`, and turned on per-dependency or via `--features` on the command line. A simpler, non-Cargo version of the same idea is just a `const`/`static bool` gating a branch.

Example (the pattern this codebase actually uses, in C#):
```
public static class FeatureFlags
{
    public static readonly bool XrefSuperscripts = true;
}
```
Newcomer mistake: assuming every 'flag' in a codebase is a Cargo feature — check whether it's a compile-time Cargo `[features]` switch (changes what gets COMPILED) or just a plain boolean constant (changes what RUNS); this codebase uses the latter, not the former.

`std::thread::scope(|scope| { scope.spawn(|| { ... }); ... })` — every `scope.spawn`'d closure must finish before `thread::scope` itself returns; the compiler enforces this, which is what lets those closures borrow data from the enclosing function safely.

Example:
```
let rows = std::thread::scope(|scope| -> Result<_, ArtifactError> {
    let handles: Vec<_> = chunks.into_iter()
        .map(|chunk| scope.spawn(move || process(chunk)))
        .collect();
    // join handles, combine results
});
```
Newcomer mistake: expecting `thread::scope` closures to be able to outlive the `scope` block (e.g., stashing a handle somewhere for later) — by design, they can't; that constraint is exactly what makes the borrowing safe.

`std::sync::mpsc::channel()` returns a `(Sender, Receiver)` pair; a sender can be cloned and handed to multiple threads, a receiver reads whatever arrives, in order.

Example (illustrative, not from this repo):
```
let (tx, rx) = std::sync::mpsc::channel();
tx.send(compute_something()).unwrap();
let result = rx.recv().unwrap();
```
Newcomer mistake: reaching for a channel when a simpler tool would do — if you just need EACH worker's final result back once, `thread::scope`'s own `JoinHandle::join()` (returning each spawned closure's return value directly) is simpler than setting up a channel, which is exactly why this codebase's parallel build skips channels entirely.

`async fn name(...) -> T { ... }` defines a function that returns a not-yet-run 'future'; `.await` inside another async function actually runs it and yields control back to the runtime (see `tokio`) while it's waiting.

Example:
```
pub async fn node_card(State(graph): State<Arc<GraphService>>, Path(id): Path<String>)
    -> Result<Json<NodeCardOut>, ApiError> {
    // ...
}
```
Newcomer mistake: forgetting that an `async fn` does NOTHING until it's `.await`ed (or otherwise driven by a runtime) — just calling it produces an inert future, not the result.

`#[tokio::main] async fn main() { ... }` sets up tokio's runtime and lets `main` itself be async; `#[tokio::test] async fn test_name() { ... }` does the same for individual async tests.

Example:
```
#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> anyhow::Result<()> {
    // start the axum server here
}
```
Newcomer mistake: writing `async fn main()` without `#[tokio::main]` above it (or manually building a runtime) — plain Rust has no built-in async runtime; `async` syntax alone doesn't know how to actually run anything without one attached.

`#[cfg(test)] mod tests { use super::*; #[test] fn it_works() { assert_eq!(...); } }` at the bottom of a source file — `use super::*` pulls in everything from the enclosing module, INCLUDING private items, which is exactly what an integration test in `tests/` can't do.

Example:
```
#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;
    // test fns using crate internals directly
}
```
Newcomer mistake: putting every test as a unit test out of habit, even ones that are really checking the crate's overall PUBLIC behavior — those read more clearly (and get checked more honestly) as integration tests in `tests/`, since a unit test's access to internals makes it easy to accidentally test implementation details instead of behavior.

Using the `proptest` crate: `proptest! { #[test] fn rule_holds(x in strategy1(), y in strategy2()) { assert!(some_property(x, y)); } }` — `strategy1()`/`strategy2()` describe the RANGE of random values to try, not a fixed example.

Example (shape used in this codebase):
```
proptest! {
    #[test]
    fn merge_is_associative(a in any_scene(), b in any_scene(), c in any_scene()) {
        assert_eq!(merge(merge(a.clone(), b.clone()), c.clone()), merge(a, merge(b, c)));
    }
}
```
Newcomer mistake: writing a property that's actually too weak to catch real bugs (e.g. checking a property trivially true for every input) — the value of a property test lives entirely in choosing a property that WOULD fail if the code were wrong.

Using the `criterion` crate: files under a crate's `benches/` directory, each with a `criterion_group!`/`criterion_main!` setup, run with `cargo bench` (typically in release mode, since benchmark numbers in an unoptimized debug build aren't meaningful).

Example (shape used in this codebase):
```
fn load_real() -> (Arc<AtlasData>, Arc<GraphService>) { ... }

fn bench_queries(c: &mut Criterion) {
    let (data, graph) = load_real();
    c.bench_function("node_card", |b| b.iter(|| graph.node_card(...)));
}
```
Newcomer mistake: benchmarking a debug build and trusting the numbers — debug builds skip most optimizations, so their timings don't reflect real-world performance; `criterion` benchmarks should always run against a release build.

Declared with `build = "build.rs"` under `[package]` in `Cargo.toml`; the script itself is a normal `fn main()` that can print special `cargo:` directives Cargo understands (e.g. `cargo:rerun-if-changed=...`).

Example (illustrative, not from this repo):
```
// build.rs
fn main() {
    println!("cargo:rerun-if-changed=schema.proto");
    // generate code here
}
```
Newcomer mistake: confusing a Cargo build SCRIPT (`build.rs`, a special pre-compile hook) with `server/atlas-graph/src/build.rs` in THIS codebase — that file is just an ordinary module happening to be named `build.rs`, containing the graph-artifact build PIPELINE logic, not a Cargo build script at all.

This codebase's version: a `ContentAddressed` trait requires a type to produce `canonical_bytes()`; hashing those bytes (with the kind of thing it is) produces a `Pid` (position id).

Example:
```
pub struct ContentHash(pub u64);

pub trait ContentAddressed {
    fn canonical_bytes(&self) -> Vec<u8>;
    fn position_kind(&self) -> PositionKind;
    fn pid(&self) -> Pid {
        let mut h = DefaultHasher::new();
        self.canonical_bytes().hash(&mut h);
        Pid { kind: self.position_kind(), hash: ContentHash(h.finish()) }
    }
}
```
Newcomer mistake: assuming a content hash means 'globally unique in a cryptographic sense' here — this codebase's own doc comment discloses its current hash is a 64-bit `std` hash, a 'skeleton' stand-in for a real production-grade multihash, not collision-proof at scale.

A component is a `.razor` file (see `razor`) combining HTML-like markup with C# logic in an `@code { }` block. Parent components pass data down via `[Parameter]` properties and receive events back up via `EventCallback` (see `event-callback`).

Example:
```
<div class="card">@Title</div>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Title { get; set; } = "";
}
```
Newcomer mistake: expecting a component's fields to persist across every re-render the way a class instance normally would — Blazor can recreate/reuse component instances across renders depending on the parent's structure, so state you need to survive should live in `[Parameter]`s, a service (see `state-service`), or explicit lifecycle handling, not assumed-stable private fields alone.

`@` introduces C# inside markup: `@variable`, `@(expression)`, `@if`/`@foreach`/`@switch` for control flow, and an `@code { }` block for a component's fields, properties, and methods.

Example:
```
@if (Places.Any())
{
    <ul>
        @foreach (var p in Places)
        {
            <li>@p.Name</li>
        }
    </ul>
}
```
Newcomer mistake: forgetting that razor markup compiles into ordinary C# rendering code — a stray unescaped `@` where you meant literal text needs `@@` to escape it, the same class of gotcha as needing to escape a special character in any templating language.

`IJSRuntime.InvokeAsync<T>("jsFunctionName", args)` calls into JS from C#; a C# method marked `[JSInvokable]` (often on a class instance passed to JS as a `DotNetObjectReference`) can be called FROM JavaScript back into C#.

Example:
```
using Microsoft.JSInterop;

public interface IMapEvents
{
    void OnPlaceHover(string id, double x, double y);
}
```
Newcomer mistake: assuming every JS interop call needs `await` — in Blazor WASM specifically (unlike Blazor Server), the JS runtime is in the SAME process, so synchronous interop (`IJSInProcessRuntime`) is available and used here for cheap calls like localStorage reads, per `LocalStore.cs`'s own comment.

`async Task<T> MethodAsync(...)` (or plain `async Task` for no return value) marks a pausable method; `await someTask` inside it actually waits for the result while letting the browser handle other work in the meantime.

Example:
```
public async Task<Scene> SceneTime(int from, int to)
{
    return await _http.GetFromJsonAsync<Scene>($"/api/scene?from={from}&to={to}");
}
```
Newcomer mistake: calling an async method without `await`ing it ('fire and forget') when you actually need its result — the method starts running but your code moves on immediately, often before the result you wanted is ready, and any exception it throws can go unobserved.

A singleton service (`AddSingleton` — see `dependency-injection`) with public methods/properties for reading and mutating its state, often exposing a `Changed`/`StateChanged` event components subscribe to so they know to re-render.

Example:
```
public class SavedExplorationsService
{
    public void Rename(string id, string name) =>
        _items = _items.Select(i => i.Id == id ? i with { Name = name } : i).ToList();
}
```
Newcomer mistake: registering a state service as `AddScoped` in Blazor WASM out of habit from server-side ASP.NET Core — WASM has no per-request scope, so `AddScoped` and `AddSingleton` behave almost identically there, and this codebase's own `Program.cs` comment calls out `AddSingleton` as the deliberately correct choice.

Accessed from C# via JS interop (see `js-interop`) — typically `IJSRuntime.InvokeAsync<string>("localStorage.getItem", key)` / `"localStorage.setItem"` — wrapped in a small helper so callers don't repeat the interop boilerplate.

Example:
```
internal static class LocalStore
{
    // reads/writes a JSON document under one localStorage key
}
```
Newcomer mistake: assuming localStorage is shared across devices or synced anywhere — it's strictly per-browser, per-device, per-origin; nothing written there is visible on a different machine or even a different browser on the same machine.

`new HttpClient { BaseAddress = new Uri(...) }`, then `.GetFromJsonAsync<T>("path")` for a JSON GET request, or lower-level `.GetAsync`/`.PostAsync` when you need more control over the request/response.

Example:
```
builder.Services.AddSingleton(sp => new HttpClient {
    BaseAddress = new Uri(builder.HostEnvironment.BaseAddress)
});
```
Newcomer mistake: creating a NEW `HttpClient` per request instead of reusing one — under the hood this can exhaust available network sockets under load; registering it once via dependency injection (as this codebase does) is the standard fix.

`[Fact] public void MethodName() { /* Assert.Equal(...), etc. */ }` for a single test case; `[Theory] [InlineData(1, 2)] [InlineData(3, 4)] public void Method(int a, int b) { ... }` for the same test run against multiple listed inputs.

Example:
```
[Fact]
public void ParsesTimeRange()
{
    Assert.Equal(expected, actual);
}
```
Newcomer mistake: writing several unrelated assertions crammed into one `[Fact]` — when it fails, you only learn THAT something broke, not immediately which check; smaller, single-purpose test methods pinpoint failures faster.

Typically: `using var ctx = new Bunit.TestContext(); var cut = ctx.RenderComponent<MyComponent>(parameters => parameters.Add(p => p.Title, "x")); cut.Find("button").Click();` then assert on `cut.Markup` or component state.

Example (illustrative, not from this repo):
```
var cut = ctx.RenderComponent<Counter>();
cut.Find("button").Click();
Assert.Contains("1", cut.Markup);
```
Newcomer mistake: assuming a project's C# test project automatically covers component RENDERING just because it uses xunit — xunit alone tests plain C# logic; testing actual razor component rendering needs bUnit (or an end-to-end browser test) specifically.

A `.spec.ts` file imports Playwright's test runner, opens a page, interacts with it (`page.click(...)`, `page.hover(...)`), and asserts on what actually rendered (`await expect(locator).toBeVisible()`).

Example:
```
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('reader shows a verse', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/reader');
  await expect(page.locator('.verse-card')).toBeVisible();
});
```
Newcomer mistake: writing brittle tests that depend on exact pixel positions or timing — Playwright's `expect(...).toBeVisible()`-style assertions auto-retry for a bit, which is more robust than a raw `page.waitForTimeout(...)` sleep, and should generally be preferred.

`export function name() { ... }` / `export const x = ...;` in the defining file; `import { name, x } from './file.js';` wherever it's used. A module can also be loaded directly by the browser via `<script type="module" src="...">`.

Example:
```
// geo.js
export function toVec(phi, lambda) { ... }
export function fromVec(v) { ... }
```
Newcomer mistake: mixing ES module `import`/`export` syntax with older-style scripts that expect everything as global variables (loaded via a plain `<script>` tag without `type="module"`) — the two loading models don't automatically interoperate; a module's exports are only reachable via `import`, not as globals.

Selectors target elements by tag, class, or id and apply property/value declarations: `.verse-card { padding: 8px; border-radius: 4px; }`. Stylesheets are linked from the HTML host page or imported by a component/module.

Example:
```
.legend-icon {
  width: 16px;
  height: 16px;
}
```
Newcomer mistake: fighting CSS specificity by piling on `!important` everywhere instead of understanding WHY a more specific selector elsewhere is winning — usually a sign the stylesheet's organization (or selector specificity) needs a rethink rather than a forced override.
